#### 8. Exception Handling in Tests — `ProcessOrder_EmptyOrder_ThrowsValidationError` (line 110)

```csharp
try
{
    processor.ProcessOrder(order);
    Assert.Fail("Expected an exception but none was thrown");
}
catch (ValidationException ex)
{
    Assert.AreEqual("Order must contain at least one item", ex.Message);
}
```

Manual try/catch duplicates what the framework already provides, and a wrong exception type silently propagates up.

**Fix — use MSTest's `Assert.ThrowsException<T>`:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_EmptyOrder_ThrowsValidationError()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);

    var ex = Assert.ThrowsException<ValidationException>(
        () => processor.ProcessOrder(new Order()));

    Assert.AreEqual("Order must contain at least one item", ex.Message);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** An unexpected exception type passes silently as a "test error" instead of a clear failure.

